## Why a Hillshade Can Look “Harsh”

A hillshade is just a grayscale rendering of a DEM that pretends a light source is shining on the terrain.  
MapLibre GL draws it with a **default exaggeration of 1** and a **default illumination direction of 315°** (north‑west).  
If the DEM is low‑resolution, or if the default values are left untouched, the result often looks:

* **Too contrasty** – shadows are black, highlights are white.  
* **Too directional** – one side of every ridge is bright while the other is dark.  
* **Too “flat”** – the terrain looks like a black‑and‑white photograph rather than a subtle relief.

You can tame all of that with a few simple paint‑property tweaks (and, if you want, a little blending with other layers).

---

## 1. The Minimal Hillshade Setup

```js
// 1️⃣ Add a raster‑DEM source (replace the URL with your own DEM tiles)
map.addSource('dem', {
  type: 'raster-dem',
  url: 'https://example.com/tiles/{z}/{x}/{y}.png',
  tileSize: 256,               // most DEM tiles are 256 px
  maxzoom: 14,                 // limit to the resolution of your DEM
  // optional: improve visual quality
  rasterResampling: 'linear'   // smoother when zoomed
});

// 2️⃣ Add a hillshade layer that uses that source
map.addLayer({
  id: 'hillshade',
  type: 'hillshade',
  source: 'dem',
  paint: {
    // ← we’ll tweak these next
  }
});
```

That alone will give you a hillshade that looks exactly like the default MapLibre demo – bright on the north‑west side, dark on the opposite side, with full contrast.

---

## 2. Soften the Harshness – Paint Properties You Need

| Paint property | What it does | Typical “soft” values |
|----------------|--------------|----------------------|
| **`hillshade-exaggeration`** | Multiplies the terrain slope before shading. Lower = flatter, smoother shadows. | `0.3 – 0.6` (default = 1) |
| **`hillshade-illumination-direction`** | Azimuth of the virtual sun (degrees clockwise from north). | Anything between `0` and `360`; try `225` (south‑west) for a softer look. |
| **`hillshade-illumination-anchor`** | Whether the sun follows the map (`viewport`) or stays fixed on the globe (`map`). | `viewport` (default) is fine; keep it unless you need a rotating sun. |
| **`hillshade-shadow-color`** | Color of the shadows. Use a semi‑transparent dark tone instead of pure black. | `'rgba(0,0,0,0.45)'` |
| **`hillshade-highlight-color`** | Color of the illuminated faces. Use a semi‑transparent light tone instead of pure white. | `'rgba(255,255,255,0.35)'` |
| **`hillshade-shadow-color`** (yes, listed twice) | Same as above – you can also set a separate “accent” color for the darkest shadows. | `'rgba(0,0,0,0.6)'` |
| **`raster-opacity`** (applies to the whole layer) | Overall opacity of the hillshade. | `0.7` (or lower) to let a base map show through. |

### Example – A Soft, Subtle Hillshade

```js
map.setPaintProperty('hillshade', 'hillshade-exaggeration', 0.45);
map.setPaintProperty('hillshade', 'hillshade-illumination-direction', 225);
map.setPaintProperty('hillshade', 'hillshade-shadow-color', 'rgba(0,0,0,0.45)');
map.setPaintProperty('hillshade', 'hillshade-highlight-color', 'rgba(255,255,255,0.30)');
map.setPaintProperty('hillshade', 'raster-opacity', 0.65);
```

You can also set them all at once when you add the layer:

```js
map.addLayer({
  id: 'hillshade',
  type: 'hillshade',
  source: 'dem',
  paint: {
    'hillshade-exaggeration': 0.45,
    'hillshade-illumination-direction': 225,
    'hillshade-shadow-color': 'rgba(0,0,0,0.45)',
    'hillshade-highlight-color': 'rgba(255,255,255,0.30)',
    'raster-opacity': 0.65
  }
});
```

---

## 3. Blend the Hillshade with Other Layers

A hillshade is often used **under** a vector basemap (roads, labels, etc.) or **over** a satellite image. The trick is to control the order (`before:`) and the opacity.

```js
// Put the hillshade just above the background but below all vector layers
map.addLayer({
  id: 'hillshade',
  type: 'hillshade',
  source: 'dem',
  paint: { /* … */ }
}, 'waterway-label');   // insert before the first label layer
```

If you want the hillshade to *mix* with a satellite raster, you can add a second raster layer on top and use the `raster-blend-mode` property (supported in recent MapLibre builds):

```js
map.addLayer({
  id: 'satellite',
  type: 'raster',
  source: 'sat',
  paint: {
    'raster-opacity': 0.8,
    'raster-blend-mode': 'multiply'   // darkens the satellite where the hillshade is dark
  }
}, 'hillshade');
```

---

## 4. Optional: Smoothing the DEM Itself

If the DEM tiles are low‑resolution (e.g., 30 m or 90 m) you’ll see blocky shadows. You can mitigate that without re‑tiling the DEM:

```js
map.addSource('dem', {
  type: 'raster-dem',
  url: 'https://example.com/tiles/{z}/{x}/{y}.png',
  tileSize: 256,
  rasterResampling: 'linear',   // bilinear interpolation when zoomed
  // For newer MapLibre versions you can also enable:
  // 'raster-contrast': 0.9,
  // 'raster-brightness-min': 0.2,
  // 'raster-brightness-max': 0.8,
});
```

These raster‑level properties affect the *source* before the hillshade shading is applied, giving a smoother base.

---

## 5. Quick “Live‑Tuning” Workflow

When you’re not sure which numbers look best, use the MapLibre **debug console** (press `Ctrl+Shift+I` → Console) and run:

```js
// Change a property on the fly
map.setPaintProperty('hillshade', 'hillshade-exaggeration', 0.4);
map.setPaintProperty('hillshade', 'hillshade-illumination-direction', 200);
map.setPaintProperty('hillshade', 'raster-opacity', 0.6);
```

Refresh the map (or just pan/zoom) and see the effect instantly. Keep tweaking until the hillshade feels “soft” rather than “harsh”.

---

## 6. Full Minimal Example (Copy‑Paste)

```html
<!DOCTYPE html>
<html>
<head>
  <meta charset="utf-8" />
  <title>Soft Hillshade – MapLibre GL JS</title>
  <script src="https://unpkg.com/maplibre-gl@4.2.0/dist/maplibre-gl.js"></script>
  <link href="https://unpkg.com/maplibre-gl@4.2.0/dist/maplibre-gl.css" rel="stylesheet" />
  <style>
    body, html { margin:0; padding:0; height:100%; }
    #map { width:100%; height:100%; }
  </style>
</head>
<body>
<div id="map"></div>
<script>
  const map = new maplibregl.Map({
    container: 'map',
    style: {
      version: 8,
      sources: {},
      layers: []
    },
    center: [-122.447303, 37.753574],
    zoom: 12
  });

  map.on('load', () => {
    // 1️⃣ DEM source (replace with your own DEM tiles)
    map.addSource('dem', {
      type: 'raster-dem',
      url: 'https://tileserver.example.com/dem/{z}/{x}/{y}.png',
      tileSize: 256,
      maxzoom: 14,
      rasterResampling: 'linear'
    });

    // 2️⃣ Soft hillshade layer
    map.addLayer({
      id: 'hillshade',
      type: 'hillshade',
      source: 'dem',
      paint: {
        'hillshade-exaggeration': 0.45,
        'hillshade-illumination-direction': 225,
        'hillshade-shadow-color': 'rgba(0,0,0,0.45)',
        'hillshade-highlight-color': 'rgba(255,255,255,0.30)',
        'raster-opacity': 0.65
      }
    }, 'waterway-label'); // insert just before the first label layer
  });
</script>
</body>
</html>
```

*Replace the `url` with a real raster‑DEM endpoint (e.g., a Mapbox DEM, a USGS 3‑arc‑second DEM, or any XYZ tile service that returns elevation data.)*

---

## TL;DR Checklist

| ✅ | Action |
|---|--------|
| **Load a raster‑DEM source** (not a normal raster). |
| **Add a `hillshade` layer** that points to that source. |
| **Reduce `hillshade-exaggeration`** to 0.3‑0.6. |
| **Change the sun direction** (`hillshade-illumination-direction`) to something other than the default 315°, e.g., 225°. |
| **Make shadows and highlights semi‑transparent** (`rgba(...,0.xx)`). |
| **Lower the overall opacity** (`raster-opacity` ≈ 0.6) if you want the base map to show through. |
| **Optionally smooth the DEM** with `rasterResampling: 'linear'`. |
| **Blend with other layers** (order, `raster-blend-mode`, etc.). |
| **Iterate live** with `map.setPaintProperty()` until it looks right. |

Follow those steps and your hillshade will go from “harsh, high‑contrast terrain” to a **subtle, natural‑looking relief** that enhances the map without overwhelming it. Happy mapping!
